An abstract formatter primitive answers rather than raises.

NSFormatter is abstract, but a subclass is entitled to call super for the cases it does not handle
itself, and Swift Publisher does exactly that from -[CCMeasurementUnitsFormatter
stringForObjectValue:], through objc_msgSendSuper2, while formatting the ruler of a document that
has a selected page. Ours raised NSRequestConcreteImplementation there.

The raise did not stay local. It happened under the Wayland keyboard focus callback, which is
extern C and cannot unwind, so Rust aborted the whole process the instant the window was given
focus. An application that ships on macOS is not crashing on this path, so the base class answers
nil and lets the caller decide.

--- a/src/NSFormatter.m
+++ b/src/NSFormatter.m
@@ -13,9 +13,21 @@
 
 @implementation NSFormatter
 
+/*
+ * NIL, NOT A RAISE.
+ *
+ * NSFormatter is abstract, but its primitives ANSWER rather than raise: a subclass is entitled to
+ * call super for the cases it does not handle itself. Swift Publisher does exactly that, from
+ * -[CCMeasurementUnitsFormatter stringForObjectValue:] through objc_msgSendSuper2, while formatting
+ * the ruler of a document that has a selected page.
+ *
+ * Raising there took the whole process down rather than the one call, because the exception was
+ * raised under the Wayland keyboard focus callback, which is extern C and cannot unwind, so Rust
+ * aborted. An application that ships on macOS is not crashing on this path, so the base class
+ * answers nil and the caller decides what to do about it.
+ */
 - (NSString *)stringForObjectValue:(id)obj
 {
-    NSRequestConcreteImplementation();
     return nil;
 }
 
